This project presents the design and development of a glue-dispensing jig system for the assembly of a novel microchannel polymer heat exchanger (MPHX). The system is intended to automate and standardize the process of applying adhesive to ABS MPHX plates and bond them to ABS lamina, improving consistency, throughput, and reliability. Key design requirements include achieving a high throughput rate (1,000 modules per heat exchanger within an 8-hour workday), ensuring strong adhesive bonds capable of withstanding internal pressures exceeding 10 psig, and maintaining precise alignment during assembly.

To meet these requirements, a roller-based adhesive application system was selected due to its ability to uniformly distribute a high-viscosity solvent-based adhesive. The system integrates three primary subsystems: an adhesive roller assembly, a jig system for alignment and handling of plates and lamina, and a feed mechanism. Overall, the design provides a robust and efficient solution for semi-automated MPHX module assembly with potential for future commercial application.